 "title": "The Psychology of Pricing",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator claims that the psychology of pricing is weird because things feel expensive or cheap based on what you compare them to, not the headline price. He explains how installment services like Klarna alter consumer perception of spending, and admits he falls for these biases himself despite studying them.",
 "key_points": [
  "Dropping the price is the last thing you should try.",
  "The psychology of price has nothing to do with the headline price; it depends entirely on what you compare it to.",
  "The creator claims Indian food is massively underpriced in the UK because people happy to spend 120 pounds on a French meal get upset about paying 40 pounds for an Indian meal.",
  "People do not ask if something is expensive; they always ask \"compared to what?\".",
  "Services like Klarna make three payments of 100 pounds feel completely different from one payment of 300 pounds.",
  "The creator admits he has studied buyer biases for ages, but these pricing tricks still work on him."
 ],
 "content": "Why Dropping Prices Is The Worst Thing You Can Do. Dropping the price is the last thing you should try. The psychology of price is really weird because things can feel expensive or feel cheap and it's got nothing to do with the headline price, it's what you're comparing it to. I always thought by the way, Indian food was massively underpriced in the UK because people who'd happily spend 120 pounds on a French meal, would get really pissy if they went out for an Indian meal and had to pay 40 pounds even though it's obviously better food. People don't really have an answer to the question \"is it expensive?\", they always go \"compared to what?\". I mean we all know this, okay, how many people use Klarna here, come on admit it. The joke hours make about Klarna is that three payments of 100 pounds feels completely different from one payment of 300 pounds. Once buying one of those Dyson air purifiers, and I get to the final bit where you have to pay and it goes 450 quid, and I go not paying that. Then it says, or you can have three payments of 150 pounds and I go, oh yeah that's perfectly reasonable. And I went and bought it. Now I've studied all these biases and things myself for ages, but it still worked on me.",
 "status": "done",
